Your project follows a structured Page Object Model (POM) with Cucumber BDD and includes
 essential automation framework components like hooks, domain objects, custom types, and step definitions.

🏗 How Everything is Connected in the Framework
A veteran automation tester would explain the project to a novice in the following way:

🔹 1. Feature Files (Cucumber BDD)
📍 Location: resources/features/
📌 Purpose: Defines test scenarios in plain English (Gherkin syntax) so that non-technical
stakeholders can understand the test cases.
🔗 Connected to: Step Definitions (stepDefinitions/)

📌 Example:

gherkin
Copy
Edit
Scenario: Guest Order Using Default Payment Option
    Given I am a Guest User
    And I have the below details
    | firstName | lastName | country | address_line1 | city | state | zipCode | emailID |
    | Marshal   | Mathers  | India   | Manjri Khurd  | Pune | Kansas | 75024   | andlf@gmail |
    And I have added a product from the cart
    And I am on the Checkout page
    When I provide the shipping details
    And I place the order
    Then the order should be placed successfully
➡ The step definitions will have corresponding methods that implement this logic.

🔹 2. Step Definitions
📍 Location: stepDefinitions/
📌 Purpose: Maps Gherkin steps from feature files to actual Java methods that interact with the application.
🔗 Connected to: Page Objects (pageObjects/)

📌 Example: (from Guest_Place_Order_Steps.java)

java
Copy
Edit
@Given("I have the below details")
public void i_have_the_below_details(DataTable table) {
    List<BillingDetails> billingDetails = table.asList(BillingDetails.class);
    checkoutPage.enterBillingDetails(billingDetails.get(0));
}
➡ Uses Cucumber DataTables to pass structured test data.

🔹 3. Page Objects (POM)
📍 Location: pageObjects/
📌 Purpose: Provides a layer of abstraction for interacting with UI elements, ensuring
 maintainability and reusability.
🔗 Connected to: Step Definitions (stepDefinitions/), DriverFactory (factory/)

📌 Example: (from CheckoutPage.java)

java
Copy
Edit
public class CheckoutPage {
    private WebDriver driver;

    @FindBy(id = "billingFirstName")
    private WebElement firstNameField;

    @FindBy(id = "billingLastName")
    private WebElement lastNameField;

    public CheckoutPage(WebDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
        PageFactory.initElements(driver, this);
    }

    public void enterBillingDetails(BillingDetails details) {
        firstNameField.sendKeys(details.getFirstName());
        lastNameField.sendKeys(details.getLastName());
    }
}
➡ Encapsulates UI actions to improve maintainability.

